    "passage": "Trans fat is unsaturated fat which is altered by partial hydrogenation. The process of partial hydrogenation forces the oils which are [[1]] liquid at room temperature to become solid. Therefore, the fat is [[2]] and becomes similar to saturated fat. Like saturated fats, trans fats raise levels of LDL cholesterol—the so-called “bad” cholesterol. Some research indicates that they may also decrease level of HDL—the so-called “good” cholesterol.\nFood which are major [[3]] to trans fatty acid intake are baked goods such as doughnuts and pastries. Snack chips and cookies also contain high amounts of trans fat [[4]]. Research has correlated trans fat intake with heart disease, diabetes, and immune dysfunction. It should be mentioned that trans fat is not bad in [[5]]. In fact, it is [[6]] intake which will more than likely be the cause of problems. Eating foods high in trans fats [[7]] may adversely affect your cholesterol levels, which is not recommended by doctors. It is [[8]] for those trying to lose weight to understand this fat because replacing saturated fats [[9]] with trans fat could result in weight gain. Unfortunately, some obstacles have prevented companies from fully abandoning trans fats. Oil with trans fat has many characteristics that are hard to [[10]] without adding saturated fat. As a result, many companies are still in the process of testing trans fat-free oils.",
